[{"id":"29221","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"754","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The solute-like component or the dispersed particles in a colloid form the ______.</span></p>","option_1":"A) solvent","option_2":"B) dispersed phase","option_3":"C) continuous phase","option_4":"D) solution phase","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - dispersed phase.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>In a colloidal solution, there are two phases — the dispersed phase (the solute-like substance broken into small particles) and the dispersed medium/continuous phase (in which these particles are spread). The particles themselves, ranging in size from 1 to 1000 nm, constitute the dispersed phase, e.g. fat particles dispersed in milk.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29222","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"754","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>What is the meaning of the term Hiranyagarbha?</span></p>","option_1":"A) Sacred fire","option_2":"B) Golden womb","option_3":"C) Divine offering","option_4":"D) Royal coronation","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - Golden womb.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Hiranyagarbha literally means 'Golden Womb' or 'Golden Egg' and refers to the source of creation of the universe in Vedic and Puranic cosmology. It is also the name of a ritual (Hiranyagarbha Dana/Yajna) performed by kings to claim higher social status, historically performed by rulers such as the Chalukyas and other medieval Indian dynasties </span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29223","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"754","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Raziya Sultana was removed from the throne in which year?</span></p>","option_1":"A) 1240","option_2":"B) 1244","option_3":"C) 1248","option_4":"D) 1252","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - 1240.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Raziya Sultana, daughter of Iltutmish and the only woman to rule the Delhi Sultanate, ascended the throne in 1236 CE. She was deposed and killed in 1240 CE following a rebellion by the Turkish nobles (the 'Chihalgani' or Group of Forty), who resented being ruled by a woman.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29224","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"754","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Which of the following five year plan of India was launched after the initiation of structural adjustment policies?</span></p>","option_1":"A) First","option_2":"B) Second","option_3":"C) Third","option_4":"D) Eighth","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - Eighth.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>India's structural adjustment programme (economic liberalisation, privatisation, and globalisation reforms) began in 1991 under P.V. Narasimha Rao's government. The Eighth Five Year Plan (1992–1997) was the first plan launched after these reforms, marking a shift from centralised planning towards a more market-oriented economy. </span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29225","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"754","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Which of the following philosopher was born in Tamil Nadu in the eleventh century?</span></p>","option_1":"A) Chaitanya","option_2":"B) Ramanuja","option_3":"C) Shankara","option_4":"D) Namdev","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - Ramanuja.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Ramanuja (1017–1137 CE), the great Vaishnavite philosopher and propounder of Vishishtadvaita (qualified non-dualism), was born at Sriperumbudur near modern Chennai in Tamil Nadu in the 11th century. He is distinguished from Adi Shankaracharya (8th century, born in Kerala, propounder of Advaita)</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29226","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"754","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Which of the following layer of atmosphere helps in radio transmission?</span></p>","option_1":"A) Troposphere","option_2":"B) Stratosphere","option_3":"C) Mesosphere","option_4":"D) Thermosphere","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - Thermosphere.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Thermosphere contains the ionosphere, a region of electrically charged particles (ions) that reflects radio waves back to Earth, enabling long-distance radio transmission and communication. </span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29227","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"754","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The time period between the 6th and 4th centuries BCE is associated with the rise of ______.</span></p>","option_1":"A) Magadha","option_2":"B) Guptas","option_3":"C) Lodis","option_4":"D) Mughals","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Magadha.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The period from the 6th to 4th centuries BCE, often called the age of the Mahajanapadas, saw the rise of Magadha as the most powerful kingdom in ancient India under rulers like Bimbisara, Ajatashatru, and the Nanda dynasty, eventually paving the way for the Mauryan Empire. </span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29228","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"754","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>'Naurata' dance during Navratri is performed in which region?</span></p>","option_1":"A) Marwar","option_2":"B) Vidarbha","option_3":"C) Konkan","option_4":"D) Bundelkhand","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - Bundelkhand.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>'Naurata' (also spelled Navrata) is a folk dance performed during Navratri in the Bundelkhand region of Madhya Pradesh and Uttar Pradesh. It is performed by women in a circular formation to celebrate the nine nights of Navratri</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29229","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"754","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>In Humans, involuntary actions including blood pressure, salivation and vomiting are controlled by the ______ in the ______.</span></p>","option_1":"A) Medulla, hind-brain","option_2":"B) Cerebrum, mid-brain","option_3":"C) Spinal cord, fore-brain","option_4":"D) Medulla, mid-brain","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Medulla, hind-brain.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Medulla Oblongata, part of the hindbrain, controls involuntary vital functions such as heart rate, blood pressure, breathing, salivation, swallowing, and vomiting. </span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29230","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"754","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Which of the following festivals is celebrated in the Union Territory of Ladakh?</span></p>","option_1":"A) Karaga","option_2":"B) Shigmo","option_3":"C) Yuru Kabgyat","option_4":"D) Thrissur Pooram","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - Yuru Kabgyat.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Yuru Kabgyat is a masked-dance festival celebrated at the Hemis Monastery in Ladakh, commemorating the victory of good over evil and the birth anniversary of Guru Padmasambhava. Karaga is a Karnataka festival, Shigmo is celebrated in Goa, and Thrissur Pooram is celebrated in Kerala </span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29231","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"754","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Which lake in Himachal Pradesh is associated with Baisakhi celebrations?</span></p>","option_1":"A) Loktak Lake","option_2":"B) Chilika Lake","option_3":"C) Pushkar Lake","option_4":"D) Rewalsar Lake","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - Rewalsar Lake.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Rewalsar Lake (also called 'Tso Pema') in Mandi district, Himachal Pradesh, is a sacred lake associated with Baisakhi celebrations and is revered by Hindus, Buddhists, and Sikhs alike. Loktak Lake is in Manipur, Chilika Lake in Odisha, and Pushkar Lake in Rajasthan </span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29232","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"754","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>In which of the following years was the census not conducted in Jammu &amp; Kashmir?</span></p>","option_1":"A) 1981","option_2":"B) 1991","option_3":"C) 2001","option_4":"D) 2011","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - 1991.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The 1991 Census of India could not be conducted in Jammu &amp; Kashmir due to militancy and disturbed conditions in the state at the time. </span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29233","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"754","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The International Space Station was launched in ______.</span></p>","option_1":"A) 1965","option_2":"B) 1972","option_3":"C) 1998","option_4":"D) 2004","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - 1998.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The first module of the International Space Station (ISS), Zarya, was launched on 20 November 1998, followed by continuous assembly involving the USA, Russia, and other partner space agencies. </span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29234","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"754","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The International Space Station travels from west to east on an orbital inclination of ______ degrees.</span></p>","option_1":"A) 51.6","option_2":"B) 184.3","option_3":"C) 234.6","option_4":"D) 312.5","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - 51.6.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The ISS orbits Earth at an inclination of approximately 51.6 degrees to the equator, travelling west to east, completing an orbit roughly every 90 minutes at an altitude of about 400 km. </span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29235","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"754","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>In 1930, ______ established the Depressed Classes Association.</span></p>","option_1":"A) Mahatma Gandhi","option_2":"B) B.R. Ambedkar","option_3":"C) Jyoti Rao Phule","option_4":"D) M. G. Ranade","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - B.R. Ambedkar.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Dr. B.R. Ambedkar established the Depressed Classes Association (Bahishkrit Hitakarini Sabha's later associated body) in 1930 to fight for the social and political rights of the depressed classes (Dalits). </span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29236","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"754","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>What is the primary purpose of the 'Bhargavastra system' developed in May 2025?</span></p>","option_1":"A) Anti-submarine warfare","option_2":"B) Countering swarm drone threats","option_3":"C) Surface-to-air missile for fighter jets","option_4":"D) Missile defense against ballistic missiles","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - Countering swarm drone threats.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Bhargavastra is an indigenously developed low-cost counter-drone system unveiled in May 2025, designed to detect and neutralise swarm drone attacks using micro-rocket-based munitions. It reflects India's growing defence indigenisation efforts and is a current-affairs based science &amp; technology/defence question frequently seen in recent SSC exams.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29237","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"754","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>A ______ is a disturbance that moves through a medium when the particles of the medium set neighbouring particles into motion.</span></p>","option_1":"A) frequency","option_2":"B) wave","option_3":"C) phase","option_4":"D) tone","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - wave.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>A wave is defined as a disturbance that travels through a medium by transferring energy from one particle to the neighbouring particle without the particles themselves undergoing net displacement. Frequency, phase, and tone are properties/characteristics associated with waves, not the disturbance itself </span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29238","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"754","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The 'EnVision' mission of the European Space Agency is aimed at exploring which planet?</span></p>","option_1":"A) Mars","option_2":"B) Venus","option_3":"C) Jupiter","option_4":"D) Mercury","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - Venus.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>EnVision is a European Space Agency (ESA) mission, planned for launch in the early 2030s, aimed at studying Venus in detail — its surface, subsurface, and atmosphere — to understand its geological evolution. Current space-mission-based questions like this are increasingly common in the SSC science &amp; technology section.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29239","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"754","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The real gross domestic product (GDP) is calculated in a way such that, the goods and services are evaluated at ______.</span></p>","option_1":"A) some constant set of prices","option_2":"B) prices set by world trade organisation","option_3":"C) prices set by International monetary fund","option_4":"D) the lowest prices observed in the economy","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - some constant set of prices.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Real GDP measures the value of goods and services produced in an economy using a constant/base-year set of prices, thereby removing the effect of inflation and allowing comparison of output across years. This is distinguished from Nominal GDP, which uses current-year prices </span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29240","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"754","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Ruhr Coal-field is located in which of the following countries?</span></p>","option_1":"A) Germany","option_2":"B) China","option_3":"C) Russia","option_4":"D) France","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Germany.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Ruhr coalfield, located in the Ruhr river valley in western Germany, is one of the largest coal-producing and industrial regions in Europe, historically forming the base of Germany's heavy industry. </span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29241","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"754","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>In the context of Privatisation, what is the meaning of total decentralisation?</span></p>","option_1":"A) It implies 100% transfer of ownership of a public enterprise to private sector.","option_2":"B) It implies 51% transfer of ownership of a private enterprise to public sector.","option_3":"C) It implies 51% transfer of ownership of a public enterprise to private sector.","option_4":"D) It implies 100% transfer of ownership of a private enterprise to public sector.","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - It implies 100% transfer of ownership of a public enterprise to private sector..</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Total decentralisation (or complete/100% privatisation) refers to the entire transfer of ownership and management of a public sector enterprise to the private sector, giving the private entity full control. Partial privatisation, in contrast, involves transfer of a portion (e.g. 51% or less) of ownership. </span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29242","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"754","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Which book of poems was written by Sarojini Naidu and published in 1905?</span></p>","option_1":"A) The Indian War of Independence","option_2":"B) The Story of My Deportation","option_3":"C) Glimpses of World History","option_4":"D) The Golden Threshold","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - The Golden Threshold.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>'The Golden Threshold' (1905) was Sarojini Naidu's first collection of poems, which established her reputation as a poet and earned her the title 'Nightingale of India'. 'The Indian War of Independence' was written by V.D. Savarkar, and 'Glimpses of World History' by Jawaharlal Nehru </span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29243","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"754","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Ekattarso Mahadeva Temple is also known as ______.</span></p>","option_1":"A) Chausath Yogini Temple","option_2":"B) Virupaksha Temple","option_3":"C) Somnath Temple","option_4":"D) Dilwara Temple","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Chausath Yogini Temple.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Ekattarso Mahadeva Temple, located in Kanker district of Chhattisgarh, is also known as the Chausath Yogini Temple, dedicated to Shiva along with 64 Yoginis. </span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29244","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"754","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>A typical example of early cave architecture is the Lomas Rishi cave in the Barabar Hills of ______.</span></p>","option_1":"A) Kerala","option_2":"B) Jharkhand","option_3":"C) Gujarat","option_4":"D) Bihar","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - Bihar.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Lomas Rishi Cave, part of the Barabar Hill Caves in Bihar's Jehanabad district, dates to the Mauryan period (3rd century BCE) and is famous for its ornamental facade resembling later chaitya-hall entrances, making it an early example of Indian rock-cut cave architecture</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29245","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"754","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Which of the following statement is correct regarding thumri?</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>I. The thumri is very lyrical in its structure and presentation.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>II. Thumri is a love song and hence the textual beauty is very important.</span></p>","option_1":"A) Only I","option_2":"B) Only II","option_3":"C) Both I and II","option_4":"D) Neither I nor II","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - Both I and II.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Thumri is a semi-classical vocal form of Hindustani music, known for its lyrical (melodious, flowing) style of presentation. It is predominantly a genre of love/romantic songs (often devotional love themes such as Radha-Krishna), where the beauty and meaning of the text (lyrics) hold great importance alongside the music. Hence, both statements I and II are correct </span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""}]